        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hypercalcemia—15–30% of cases; associated hyposthenuria.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Secondary renal failure may develop with longstanding and severe hypercalcemia. Beware of interpreting urine specific gravity to differentiate prerenal versus renal azotemia in hypercalcemic patients. Hypercalcemia inhibits concentrating abilities of kidneys, thus azotemia with dilute urine may be seen with both prerenal and renal azotemia. Treatment of disease to resolve hypercalcemia and fluid therapy is needed to determine if underlying renal pathology is present.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "canonical_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "If hypercalcemia is present, and tumor cannot be identified, parathyroid hormone and PTHrP levels can be assessed—high PTHrP supports neoplasia as cause of hypercalcemia. This ancillary screening test is not commonly required, as anal gland tumors are easily palpable.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "IMAGING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "IMAGING",
          "canonical_heading": "IMAGING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Abdominal radiography—to evaluate medial iliac lymph nodes and lumbar and pelvic bones.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Thoracic radiography—to evaluate for pulmonary metastasis.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Abdominal ultrasonography—may identify enlarged locoregional lymph nodes not visible radiographically, and also metastatic lesions involving visceral organs (liver/spleen).",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CT scan—identifies lymphadenopathy with greater sensitivity than ultrasound. In one study ultrasound found all affected lymph nodes in only 30.8% of cases where CT identified multiple nodes involved.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Fine-needle aspiration and cytology of anal sac mass to rule out conditions other than adenocarcinoma; while differentiation of benign versus malignant neoplasm of perianal masses is difficult, apocrine gland adenocarcinoma of anal sac will have neuroendocrine appearance and can be differentiated from perianal gland tumors.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Fine-needle aspiration and cytology of enlarged lymph nodes, liver, or splenic nodules to confirm metastasis.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Histopathology required for definitive diagnosis. Incisional biopsy can be considered, but excisional biopsy appropriate if location of mass and cytology supportive of anal sac neoplasia.",
              "pdf_page": 111,
              "printed_page": 29,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
